1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,680
Adam: So, Michelle, shall we make a start on our presentation

2
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:06,190
We haven't got that much time left.

3
00:00:06,190 --> 00:00:10,534
Michelle: No, Adam. But at least we've done all the background reading.

4
00:00:10,534 --> 00:00:12,220
I found it really interesting

5
00:00:12,740 --> 00:00:15,880
I'd never even heard of the Laki eruption before this.

6
00:00:15,880 --> 00:00:20,460
Adam: Me neither. I suppose 1783 is a long time ago.

7
00:00:20,460 --> 00:00:25,380
Michelle: But it was a huge eruption and it had such devastating consequences.

8
00:00:25,380 --> 00:00:30,713
Adam: I know. It was great there were so many primary sources to look at.

9
00:00:30,713 --> 00:00:34,980
It really gives you a sense of how catastrophic the volcano was.

10
00:00:34,980 --> 00:00:39,600
People were really trying to make sense of the science for the first time.

11
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:45,636
Michelle: That's right. But what I found more significant was how it impacted directly

12
00:00:45,636 --> 00:00:47,660
and indirectly on political events

13
00:00:47,660 --> 00:00:51,270
as well as having massive social and economic consequences.

14
00:00:51,270 --> 00:00:55,440
Adam: I know. That should be the main focus of our presentation.

15
00:00:55,440 --> 00:00:59,910
Michelle: The observations made by people at the time were interesting, weren't they?

16
00:00:59,910 --> 00:01:04,574
I mean, they all gave a pretty consistent account of what happened,

17
00:01:04,574 --> 00:01:08,460
even if they didn't always use the same terminology.

18
00:01:08,460 --> 00:01:10,940
Adam: Yeah. I was surprised there were

19
00:01:10,940 --> 00:01:13,420
so many weather stations established by that time -

20
00:01:13,420 --> 00:01:18,050
so, you know, you can see how the weather changed, often by the hour.

21
00:01:18,050 --> 00:01:26,370
Michelle: Right. Writers at the time talked about the Laki haze to describe the volcanic fog that spread across Europe.

22
00:01:26,370 --> 00:01:31,678
They all realised that this wasn't the sort of fog they were used to -

23
00:01:31,678 --> 00:01:33,860
and of course this was in pre-industrial times

24
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:37,340
so they hadn't experienced sulphur-smelling fog before.

25
00:01:37,340 --> 00:01:39,700
Adam: No, that's true.

26
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:43,886
Michelle: Reports from the period blamed the haze for an increase in headaches,

27
00:01:43,886 --> 00:01:45,496
respiratory issues and asthma attacks.

28
00:01:45,496 --> 00:01:50,970
And they all describe how it covered the sun and made it look a strange red colour.

29
00:01:50,970 --> 00:01:53,660
Adam: Must have been very weird.

30
00:01:53,660 --> 00:01:57,959
Michelle: It's interesting that Benjamin Franklin wrote about the haze.

31
00:01:57,959 --> 00:02:03,430
Did you read that? He was the American ambassador in Paris at the time.

32
00:02:03,430 --> 00:02:09,630
Adam: Yeah. At first no one realised that the haze was caused by the volcanic eruption in Iceland.

33
00:02:09,630 --> 00:02:13,890
Michelle: It was Benjamin Franklin who realised that before anyone else.

34
00:02:13,890 --> 00:02:16,130
Adam: He's often credited with that, apparently.

35
00:02:16,130 --> 00:02:19,120
But a French naturalist beat him to it

36
00:02:19,340 --> 00:02:22,010
I can't remember his name. I'd have to look it up.

37
00:02:22,010 --> 00:02:27,560
Then other naturalists had the same idea - all independently of each other.

38
00:02:27,560 --> 00:02:33,634
Michelle: Oh right. We should talk about the immediate impact of the eruption,

39
00:02:33,634 --> 00:02:39,240
which was obviously enormous - especially in Iceland, where so many people died.

40
00:02:39,240 --> 00:02:41,329
Adam: Mmm. You'd expect that -

41
00:02:41,329 --> 00:02:45,100
and the fact that the volcanic ash drifted so swiftly

42
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:48,640
but not that the effects would go on for so long.

43
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:51,119
Or that two years after the eruption,

44
00:02:51,119 --> 00:02:56,430
strange weather events were being reported as far away as North America and North Africa.

45
00:02:56,430 --> 00:03:02,963
Michelle: No. I found all that hard to believe too. It must have been terrible -

46
00:03:02,963 --> 00:03:05,691
and there was nothing anyone could do about it,

47
00:03:06,554 --> 00:03:12,110
even if they knew the ash cloud was coming in their direction

48
00:03:12,110 --> 00:03:18,400
Michelle: We should run through some of the terrible consequences of the eruption experienced in different countries.

49
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:20,620
There's quite a varied range.

50
00:03:20,620 --> 00:03:25,560
Adam: Starting with Iceland, where the impact on farming was devastating.

51
00:03:25,560 --> 00:03:32,530
Michelle: Mmm. One of the most dramatic things there was the effect on livestock as they grazed in the fields.

52
00:03:32,530 --> 00:03:36,770
They were poisoned because they ate vegetation that had been contaminated

53
00:03:36,770 --> 00:03:40,240
with fluorine as a result of the volcanic fallout.

54
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:42,890
Adam: That was horrible. In Egypt

55
00:03:43,290 --> 00:03:45,965
the bizarre weather patterns led to a severe drought

56
00:03:45,965 --> 00:03:51,690
and as a result the Nile didn't flood, which meant the crops all failed.

57
00:03:51,690 --> 00:03:55,267
Michelle: It's so far from where the eruption happened

58
00:03:55,267 --> 00:03:59,950
and yet the famine there led to more people dying than any other country

59
00:03:59,950 --> 00:04:02,420
It was worse than the plague.

60
00:04:02,420 --> 00:04:10,060
Adam: OK. Then in the UK the mortality rate went up a lot - presumably from respiratory illnesses.

61
00:04:10,060 --> 00:04:14,277
According to one report it was about double the usual number

62
00:04:14,277 --> 00:04:19,260
and included an unusually high percentage of people under the age of 25.

63
00:04:19,260 --> 00:04:27,140
Michelle: Mmm. I think people will be surprised to hear that the weather in the USA was badly affected too.

64
00:04:27,140 --> 00:04:34,524
George Washington even makes a note in his diary that they were snowbound until March in Virginia.

65
00:04:34,524 --> 00:04:37,130
That was before he became president.

66
00:04:37,130 --> 00:04:42,300
Adam: Yes, and there was ice floating down the Mississippi, which was unprecedented.

67
00:04:42,300 --> 00:04:47,310
Michelle: Astonishing, really. Anyway, what do you think...
